My lady-elf character just ascended to demigoddesshood yesterday, in what had
to be one of the strangest games I have ever played.

Warning, some spoilers follow
^L doesn't work on my terminal, so I'll leave some blank lines:






















By the time she ascended, my character had the following intrinsics:
automatic searching       (started with it)
sleep resistant           (started with it)
see invisible             (started with it)
fast                      (started fast + ate a Q + wore speed boots)
fire resistant            (ate a fire-H)
cold resistant            (ate a winter-d)
shock resistant           (ate a gelatinous-b)
disintegration resistant  (ate a black-D  -->  first time this happened to me!)
invisible                 (quaffed a blessed potion of invisibility)
reflection                (wore a shield of reflection)
could teleport            (ate a l)
teleport control          (ate a tin of t-meat)
magic resistant           (wore a cloak of magic resistance)
had extra luck +
good luck didn't time out (had a blessed luckstone)
poison resistant          (ate a cave-s)
life saved                (had 4 amulets of life saving)
caused conflict           (had a ring of conflict)
Hand of Elbereth          (sacrificed a *lot*, plus lots of luck)

and I'm probably forgetting a couple. Note that conspicuously missing from
this list is telepathy, the easiest intrinsic to gain. Throughout the
whole game, my character encountered over 20 floating eyes, not a single
one of which left a corpse! There's got to be a very low probability of
this happening! It got to the point where she considered "wasting" a wish
on a floating eye corpse, but she made do with a helm of telepathy until
a master-L blew it away in Hell. Still, 20 floating eyes is a pretty small
number to encounter in the whole game, especially after hanging around low
levels for a long time to try to find some. Another monster that was
conspicuous by its absence was the wraith. Only one was found in the whole
game. One more was created with a figurine of a wraith, but after it was
caused to die (ring of conflict) it didn't leave a corpse. My lady-elf was
willing to become monster-aggravating by eating her pet wraith in order to
gain one level, but she didn't have the chance to do it. The whole dungeon
featured only two altars and two shops, which is kind of low. All in all,
it was a strange game, but fun anyway. The ol' Wiz himself was no problem.
He got disposed of three times by one zap of death each time. It was my
first win with an Elf. I'll have to try Tourist next...

